  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/opt-in-fail-soft-live-lookup
   :name "make live vulnerability lookup opt-in and fail-soft"
   :desc "The tool runs fully offline unless a vulnerability source is selected; a failed lookup warns on stderr and yields no additional vulnerabilities instead of aborting the run."
   :doc "Rationale: network flakiness on a resource the user did not directly name should not break a CI run. Consequence and open risk: a lookup failure is indistinguishable in the report from 'no additional vulnerabilities', so a --fail-on-violations gate can pass on incomplete data (see dev/ideas.md, 'Vulnerability Database Adapters'). Contrast with the VEX decision, where malformed user-supplied files fail loudly. Evidence: dev/plans/osv-, github-advisory- and deps-dev-vulnerability-adapter.md."
   :tags #{"solution strategy"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/vex-most-conservative-status-wins
   :name "resolve conflicting VEX statements to the most conservative status"
   :desc "VEX statements are applied only via an explicit --vex-path; when several match, affected beats under-investigation beats fixed beats not-affected, so a stale not-affected claim cannot hide an affected one."
   :doc "Also: only not-affected and fixed suppress a vulnerability from the blocking report and exit code, and only for components a statement actually names. A malformed VEX file the user pointed at fails loudly, unlike live lookups. Evidence: dev/plans/vex-openvex-adapter.md, domain/vex.clj."
   :tags #{"solution strategy"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/reviewed-never-overrides-blacklist
   :name "let a license review resolve open cases but never override the blacklist"
   :desc "A reviewed component overrides grey, unidentified and no-license statuses only; blacklisted and whitelisted results stay as computed, and proprietary, reviewed or no-license components never affect the exit status."
   :doc "A real exception to the blacklist would need its own, auditable mechanism (e.g. accepted exceptions with reason and approver). Evidence: dev/plans/proprietary-components.md ('Decisions')."
   :tags #{"solution strategy"}}
  
  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/multimethod-extension-points
   :name "extend input and output formats through multimethod extension points"
   :desc "The application layer defines multimethods (repository/read-sboms, read-policies, read-vulnerability-sources, template/render); adapters register formats and sources with defmethod, so the core knows neither formats nor I/O."
   :doc "### Context

Clean Architecture requires the core to know neither SBOM formats nor I/O (decision clean-architecture-layering), while the tool must read several input formats (goal multi-format-input), render several output formats (multi-format-output) and query several vulnerability sources (live-vulnerability-lookup), with more expected.

### Options

1. Multimethods defined in the application layer, implemented by adapters.
2. Format dispatch inside the core or the CLI (cond/case on the format).
3. Protocols or records passed in as dependencies.

### Decision

Option 1. The application layer owns the dispatch point and the adapters register with defmethod.

### Consequences

A new format or source is an additive adapter namespace plus a CLI option value, and the core stays unchanged. The dependency points inward. Dispatch values are open keywords, so a typo surfaces only at runtime. The CLI stays the composition root and must require every adapter for its defmethods to be registered."
   :tags #{"architecture"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/external-vulnerabilities-by-identifier
   :name "keep live-lookup vulnerabilities outside the SBOM documents, keyed by purl"
   :desc "Vulnerabilities from live lookups are stored in the repository state (:external-vulnerabilities, purl to canonical vulnerability records) and merged per component, not injected into a synthetic SBOM document."
   :doc "### Context

A vulnerability embedded in an SBOM refers to component ids that are only meaningful within that document, so vulnerabilities are scoped per document. A live lookup is queried per purl and has no document (goal live-vulnerability-lookup).

### Options

1. Wrap lookup results in a synthetic SBOM document.
2. Keep them in separate repository state keyed by purl and merge them per component.

### Decision

Option 2. The repository state holds :external-vulnerabilities (purl to canonical vulnerability records).
Consolidating a component's vulnerabilities merges them in and deduplicates by id.

### Consequences

Reports, markdown rendering and policy evaluation treat both origins identically, and provenance stays in the per-vulnerability source field.
The merge and the repository union have to know about the second origin.
The proposed NVD adapter (dev/plans/nvd-vulnerability-adapter.md) would extend the key space with CPEs, disjoint from purls."
   :tags #{"architecture"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/shared-domain-parsers
   :name "implement purl and CVSS parsing once in the domain, without a library"
   :desc "Package URL parsing and CVSS v2/v3 base scoring are small hand-written domain namespaces shared by all vulnerability adapters, not private copies per adapter or a third-party dependency."
   :doc "### Context
Every vulnerability adapter must interpret purls (to build queries) and many sources deliver a CVSS vector instead of a severity (to rate findings). The project had no purl parser, and the first adapter carried a private one.

### Options
1. A third-party purl or CVSS library.
2. One small hand-written parser per concept in the domain layer, shared by all adapters.

### Decision

option 2. domain.purl and domain.cvss are pure domain namespaces used by all vulnerability adapters.

### Consequences

logic is tested once, adapters depend inward on the domain only, and the dependency footprint stays small. The project owns spec conformance, e.g. CVSS rounding rules, and supports only v2 and v3 for now (v4 is proposed in dev/plans/cvss-v4-vector-parser.md)."
   :tags #{"architecture"}}

