  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/typed-exceptions-with-friendly-messages
   :name "report failures as typed exceptions rendered by one CLI formatter"
   :desc "Adapters and the core throw ex-info with an :sbom-tool/error-type; the CLI turns each type into a short message and an exit code, and --debug adds the full cause chain."
   :doc "### Context

The tool is run from terminals and CI pipelines (goal ci-gate), where users need a short, actionable message and a stable exit code, not a stack trace. Failures arise in many adapters.

### Options

1. Let exceptions propagate and print them. 2. Each adapter prints its own message and exits. 3. Adapters throw ex-info carrying an error type and data, and one place in the UI adapter maps types to messages. Options 1 and 2 are reconstructed alternatives.

### Decision

Option 3. adapter.ui.errors/friendly-message is a multimethod over :sbom-tool/error-type, with an ::unknown fallback; adapter.ui.cli maps failure categories to exit codes (see decision separate-exit-codes-per-failure-category) and, with --debug, appends the cause chain (errors/debug-details).

### Consequences

Core and adapters stay free of output and exit concerns, and a new failure kind needs an error-type plus one defmethod. An error type without a defmethod falls back to a generic message. Message wording is centralized, but the error-type keywords are an implicit contract between throwers and the formatter.

### Evidence

adapter/ui/errors.clj, adapter/ui/cli.clj (-main, exit codes), 28 ex-info call sites of which 24 carry an :sbom-tool/error-type; dev/plans/cli-error-codes.md."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/failure-policy-by-input-kind
   :name "fail loudly on user-supplied inputs, degrade gracefully on remote lookups"
   :desc "A file the user names explicitly (SBOM, policy, VEX, SPDX list, key file) that is missing or malformed aborts the run; a failed remote lookup or an empty input directory only warns."
   :doc "### Context

A CI gate must not silently ignore its own inputs, but a network lookup the user did not directly name can fail for reasons outside the user's control (goals ci-gate, live-vulnerability-lookup).

### Options

1. Abort on every failure. 2. Warn and continue on every failure. 3. Distinguish by where the input comes from.

### Decision

Option 3. Explicit local inputs are as much 'the user's input' as an SBOM file and fail with a typed exception. Live vulnerability lookups (chunk or request level) warn once and resolve to no additional vulnerabilities, and an empty input directory only warns. This generalizes decision opt-in-fail-soft-live-lookup and the VEX rule in vex-most-conservative-status-wins.

### Consequences

Predictable behavior per input kind. Known weakness: a degraded lookup is indistinguishable from 'no additional vulnerabilities' in the report, so a gate can pass on incomplete data (open idea in dev/ideas.md).

### Evidence

adapter/sbom/cdx.clj and spdx.clj (empty directory warning), adapter/vex/openvex.clj, adapter/vulnerability/*.clj (warn-and-continue), adapter/policies.clj, adapter/license/spdx.clj; dev/plans/vex-openvex-adapter.md, osv-vulnerability-adapter.md."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/diagnostics-on-stderr
   :name "write reports to stdout and all diagnostics to stderr, without a logging library"
   :desc "Warnings and error messages go to *err* as plain 'Warning: ...' lines from small per-adapter helpers; stdout carries only the report."
   :doc "### Context

Reports are consumed by pipes and files, so diagnostics must not corrupt them. The tool is a short-lived CLI without long-running operations.

### Options

1. A logging library with levels and appenders. 2. Plain writes to stderr. Option 1 is a reconstructed alternative.

### Decision

Option 2. There is no logger: adapters print 'Warning: ...' to *err*, the CLI prints error messages to *err*, and --debug only adds exception detail.

### Consequences

No dependency and no configuration, and report output stays machine-readable. There are no log levels, no structured logs and no verbosity switch besides --debug. The warn helper is duplicated in several adapters.

### Evidence

adapter/sbom/cdx.clj, spdx.clj, adapter/vex/openvex.clj, adapter/vulnerability/*.clj (private warn), adapter/ui/cli.clj."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/configuration-and-secrets-handling
   :name "configure through CLI options and EDN files, and keep API keys out of the command line"
   :desc "Behavior is set by CLI options and EDN policy files validated with clojure.spec, with bundled defaults; API keys come from an EDN file or an environment variable, never from an option value."
   :doc "### Context

Policies must be configurable (goal configurable-policy). Live lookups may need API keys (goal live-vulnerability-lookup), which must not leak into shell history, process listings, logs or reports.

### Options

1. Keys as CLI option values. 2. Environment variables only. 3. A key file path option with an environment variable as fallback.

### Decision

Option 3 for keys: -K/--osv-api-key-file and -G/--github-advisory-api-key-file name an EDN file with {:api-key ...}, falling back to OSV_API_KEY and GITHUB_ADVISORY_API_KEY. Policy files are EDN, validated against a spec with a message naming the file, and a bundled default policy applies when no path is given. The SPDX license list is a file option with a bundled snapshot as default.

### Consequences

Keys never appear in the command line, and the README advises against committing key files. Policy errors are reported precisely, but policy files, key files and option names are public contracts (changes are tier 3). Network use needs explicit opt-in, so offline runs need no keys.

### Evidence

adapter/policies.clj (spec validation, bundled defaults in resources/policy), adapter/vulnerability/osv.clj and github_advisory.clj (key resolution), adapter/license/spdx.clj, adapter/ui/cli.clj options."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/provenance-and-conflict-reporting
   :name "keep provenance and report disagreements instead of silently resolving them"
   :desc "Merged components keep their sources and a :conflicts map where source documents disagree, and every report carries it; each vulnerability keeps its own source."
   :doc "### Context

One real-world component is described by several SBOM documents that may disagree (goal consolidate-components), and vulnerabilities come from SBOMs, live databases and VEX statements (goal vulnerability-reporting). Compliance reviewers need to see where a statement came from.

### Options

1. Pick one value per field (first or last wins) and drop the rest. 2. Merge and keep the disagreement visible. 3. Reject inputs that disagree.

### Decision

Option 2. domain.component computes :conflicts (field to the distinct non-blank values) and :sources when merging components; application.report/with-provenance copies them into every report row; each vulnerability carries ::sbom/source with its origin.

### Consequences

Reports stay traceable and nothing is silently overwritten, at the cost of larger report data and consumers that must handle :conflicts. Unidentified components are never merged automatically (non-goal no-automatic-unidentified-merge).

### Evidence

domain/component.clj (scalar-conflicts, merge), application/report.clj (with-provenance), adapter/vulnerability/*.clj (::sbom/source)."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

  {:el :decision
   :id :sbom-tool.decision/bounded-concurrent-lookups
   :name "run remote lookups with bounded concurrency, batching and per-run deduplication"
   :desc "Vulnerability adapters resolve each distinct identifier at most once per run, batch where the API allows, and run requests on a fixed-size thread pool."
   :doc "### Context

A real SBOM has hundreds of components, and the sources offer different capabilities and rate limits (deps.dev has no batch endpoint, OSV has a batch endpoint, GitHub has an hourly rate budget). Cross-run caching is a non-goal (no-cross-run-caching).

### Options

1. Sequential requests. 2. Unbounded parallelism (pmap or a thread per request). 3. A fixed-size pool with batching and deduplication within the run.

### Decision

Option 3. Each adapter deduplicates identifiers, chunks them to the API limits and uses a local bounded-pmap helper, a fixed-size thread pool that returns results in input order.

### Consequences

Runs finish in reasonable time without flooding the services. The helper is intentionally duplicated in the osv, deps-dev and github-advisory adapters rather than shared, so a fix has to be applied three times. Concurrency limits are constants in each adapter.

### Evidence

adapter/vulnerability/osv.clj, deps_dev.clj and github_advisory.clj (bounded-pmap); dev/plans/osv-vulnerability-adapter.md, deps-dev-vulnerability-adapter.md, github-advisory-vulnerability-adapter.md."
   :maturity :implemented
   :tags #{"crosscutting concept"}}

